-- Per-key reply order. One key is one connection.
--
-- This book is a hold rule over a fixed script. It is not the poll
-- loop, and it does not order replies across different keys.
-- `c/host.c` never calls this dispatcher. `--backend c` omits a
-- family. `--backend fsm` emits `MuroHttp.FamilyFsm`.
--
-- `accept` opens on arrival. It is never held. `reply` stays held
-- while any earlier instance of the same key is unresolved. An
-- earlier accept counts, and so does an earlier reply.
-- The pin's `agda/Muro/Family.agda` names these two kinds `init`
-- and `signal`. This book names them `accept` and `reply`.
-- `accept` is that file's `init`: it is never held. `reply` is
-- that file's `signal`: it is held while an earlier instance of
-- the same key is unresolved. The Agda names are not used here.
-- This repository does not contain that proof.
-- An instance
-- that has not arrived does not. Another key does not. An older
-- reply that arrives after a newer one has opened runs beside it.
-- This is not one reply at a time. `earlier_resolved` names the
-- earlier replies of this key known when this reply opened. It does
-- not count the reply that is opening, and it does not count an
-- accept.
--
-- The meeting is one reply: the peer puts `ok`, the host takes it,
-- and both end. It is not `session conn`. It does not classify, and
-- it does not choose a status. The host still admits one socket at
-- a time through `admitChoice` and `muro__sess_conn_step`.
--
-- `mix muro.check` walks one fixed script. Key `a` is accept, then
-- reply, then reply. Key `b` is accept. `reply-order`, `accept-free`,
-- and `reply-opens` share that graph. The counts on those lines are
-- a regression pin of the script. A different count means the
-- script, the meeting, the hold rule, or the checker explored a
-- different graph. They are not a proof for every key count, and
-- they are not a bound on live sockets. The pin proves the hold
-- rule for any number of keys and any number of instances, in that
-- package's `agda/Muro/Family.agda`. This book does not contain
-- that proof. The program that writes the dispatcher is not proved.
--
-- Not stated here. This pin has no formula for them:
--   `nconn` never exceeds `max_conn`
--   an accepted connection is eventually closed
--   a resolved connection never arrives another reply
-- The host keeps a slot until `close_slot`. The hold rule does not
-- make the host resolve. A later `reply` of a key can still open.

data Act : Type where
  accept : Act
  reply : Act

data Report : Type where
  ok : Report

session reply-out := send Report end
session reply-in := recv Report end

conform peer : reply-out := put ok done

conform host : reply-in :=
  get (r : Report) done

meet turn : host with peer buffer 1

family conns : turn per key of Act
  accept never held
  reply held while earlier unresolved

temporal drained : turn :=
  always ((host done) implies (inbox host is empty))

temporal reply-order : conns :=
  forall (k : key). always ((opened k reply) implies (earlier_resolved k reply))

temporal accept-free : conns :=
  forall (k : key). always (not (held k accept))

temporal reply-opens : conns :=
  forall (k : key). (held k reply) leads_to (opened k reply)
